# The build context is the repo root for both images, so without this every
# `docker build` uploads .venv and node_modules — hundreds of megabytes, most of
# it platform-specific binaries that would be wrong inside a Linux image anyway.

# Secrets. Neither Dockerfile copies these, but a COPY added later would, and a
# credential baked into an image layer survives every later deletion — the layer
# is still there and still pullable. This is the same class of mistake as
# `env.txt`, which reached a push through `git add -A`.
.env
.env.*
*.env.txt
env.txt
Example.env

# Virtualenvs and node_modules are rebuilt inside the image from the lockfiles.
# Copying a Windows or macOS .venv in would shadow the Linux one uv creates.
.venv
**/node_modules
**/__pycache__
**/*.pyc

# Build output and caches
.next
**/.next
**/.next-build
dist
.ruff_cache
.pytest_cache
.mypy_cache

# Local runtime state. `.agent-state` is a laptop's journal and mandates —
# baking it into the image would ship one machine's vault history to the server
# and then be shadowed by the volume mount anyway.
.agent-state
.logs
.run
broadcast
cache
out

# Version control and tooling
.git
.github
.claude

# Foundry build artifacts. `contracts/` is not copied into either image at all —
# the chain is deployed to from an operator's machine, never from a container.
contracts/out
contracts/cache
contracts/lib
venues/aqua/solidity/out
venues/aqua/solidity/cache
venues/aqua/solidity/lib
